<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The petitioner, married into the family since 2008, lived with her son on the ground floor of a Pitampura property; her parents-in-law, aged about 70 and 64, lived on the first floor; her husband lived separately. The District Magistrate refused the in-laws' eviction application in December 2025, finding separate entries and directing non-obstruction. On appeal, the Divisional Commissioner reversed in August 2026 and directed the daughter-in-law to vacate once the seniors provided alternate accommodation. The family's litigation map was dense: DV Act proceedings pending since 2018, a title suit over the property, several FIRs, and a Section 125 CrPC maintenance case in which the petitioner receives Rs 30,000 per month interim maintenance.</p>

<h2>Eviction upheld in principle</h2>
<p>The Court found no jurisdictional error or perversity in the Appellate Authority's conclusion that continued co-living had become unworkable: the DM's own inspection had found the petitioner blocking the shared staircase and terrace, and the cumulative record of conflict supported the view that the parties must separate. Relying on the line of authority including <em>S. Vanitha v. Deputy Commissioner, Bengaluru Urban</em>, (2021) 15 SCC 730, <em>Satish Chander Ahuja v. Sneha Ahuja</em>, (2021) 1 SCC 414, and the Division Bench in <em>Manju Arora v. Neelam Arora</em>, the Court restated the governing balance: eviction powers under the Senior Citizens Act exist, but they cannot be used mechanically to render a woman's DV Act residence right illusory; the two statutes must be read together and relief moulded accordingly.</p>

<h2>Where the original order fell short</h2>
<p>The Appellate Authority's direction was, the Court held, correct in principle but fatally vague: it fixed no standard of accommodation, no rent terms, no duration and no consequence of default — and gave a woman resident since 2008, with a son in Class XII, roughly three weeks to leave. The High Court rewrote the operative terms:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Identification (4 weeks):</strong> the petitioner identifies a two-bedroom residence with drawing room and kitchen, in the same or an adjoining locality, within reasonable commuting distance of her son's school.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Costs on the in-laws:</strong> rent up to Rs 35,000 per month — their own offer in court — plus security deposit, brokerage and reasonable shifting expenses.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Advance security:</strong> six months' rent in advance plus the deposit within two weeks of receiving the particulars; thereafter rent by the 10th of each month.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Vacating (2 weeks after compliance):</strong> the petitioner and her son hand over peaceful possession, with SDM assistance if required.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Duration and default:</strong> the obligation continues until her residence rights are finally decided in the DV or matrimonial proceedings; two consecutive months' default lets her seek restoration of possession from the Appellate Authority.</div>
</div>
<p>The arrangement is in addition to — not in adjustment of — the husband's maintenance obligations, and is without prejudice to the pending civil, DV and maintenance proceedings.</p>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>
<p>Most reported decisions in this field answer only the binary question — eviction or no eviction. This judgment's contribution is operational: it treats the specificity of relocation terms as part of the legality of the eviction itself. An order that says "alternate accommodation" without fixing standard, rent flow, duration and default consequences is incomplete, because the woman's statutory right would then depend on the goodwill of the very parties evicting her. Practitioners on both sides now have a template: seniors seeking eviction should come with a concrete, funded relocation offer; women resisting eviction should insist that any relocation direction carry deposits, timelines and a restoration clause.</p>
